Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["q < 3",
 FontSize->36,
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.7607843137254902, 0.29411764705882354`, 0.08235294117647059]]], "Subtitle",\

 CellChangeTimes->{{3.89325259655807*^9, 3.893252598919417*^9}, {
  3.8932560720239463`*^9, 3.893256076743604*^9}, {3.893256115992225*^9, 
  3.893256116067061*^9}, {3.89772743676551*^9, 3.897727458937772*^9}, {
  3.9379920037107267`*^9, 3.937992004860751*^9}, {3.9608995502657948`*^9, 
  3.960899550586073*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7e8046f2-339e-344d-b356-\
66eea8b2671f"],

Cell["\<\
In this section, we analyse the dynamics when q < 3. In this case, two \
separatrices exist: one between the repellor and the singularity at (1,1), \
and the other between the repellor and saddle fixed point. These separatrices \
separate two types of trajectory: one group of trajectories expand towards a \
singularity, and the other are non-singular and tend towards the attractor \
fixed point representing a cosmological constant. The separatrix between the \
repellor and saddle fixed point is also a non-singular trajectory.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.960899616621567*^9, 3.9608996358016376`*^9}, {
  3.960899702013615*^9, 3.960899754841767*^9}, {3.960907246222824*^9, 
  3.960907291863053*^9}, {3.9609607269049854`*^9, 
  3.960960840693239*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"0a973e18-7d93-1b45-94fc-\
32f5152c3809"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["2D Phase Spaces",
 FontSize->24,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[0]]], "Chapter",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8986756557416477`*^9, 3.898675658535152*^9}, {
   3.900736121951954*^9, 3.9007361666897087`*^9}, {3.900759488452829*^9, 
   3.900759491495037*^9}, {3.901008920130779*^9, 3.901008925531996*^9}, {
   3.901097307670695*^9, 3.9010973112706957`*^9}, 3.904980096358492*^9, 
   3.904980251240255*^9, {3.910440626117382*^9, 3.910440631445214*^9}, {
   3.917095447879121*^9, 3.917095449014579*^9}, {3.917095521432156*^9, 
   3.917095523076204*^9}, {3.9187240234834023`*^9, 3.9187240250606413`*^9}, {
   3.919151786886721*^9, 3.919151804744297*^9}, {3.9228718068670893`*^9, 
   3.922871807186956*^9}, 3.922872929964172*^9, {3.923561893839754*^9, 
   3.923561894568316*^9}, {3.923563347004373*^9, 3.923563351828269*^9}, 
   3.931343467909794*^9, {3.937991482861972*^9, 3.93799148371071*^9}, {
   3.937992007820413*^9, 
   3.9379920106548357`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9efa074d-449a-4643-b30e-\
102f21e53434"],

Cell["\<\
Here, we plot the 2D z - x dynamics, as well as the compact Z - X phase \
spaces, for wx > -1 and \[Epsilon] = -1. Y > 0 in these phase spaces, \
therefore the trajectories are expanding. We complete a dynamical systems \
analysis for each case when the repellor is a spiral repellor, a repelling \
node and improper node.   

We also plot the curve when the acceleration is zero (a\[CloseCurlyQuote]\
\[CloseCurlyQuote] = 0). The \[OpenCurlyQuote]curves\[CloseCurlyQuote] in the \
heading below correspond to the zero acceleration curve and the separatrix \
between the repellor and the saddle fixed point. If the separatrix and zero \
acceleration curve cross, then all non-singular models, including the \
separatrix, have a decelerated expansion phase followed by a late-time \
acceleration. If the curves do not cross, then some non-singular trajectories \
have a decelerated phase, and some always accelerate. In this case there is \
one non-singular trajectory that reaches a point of zero acceleration, but \
otherwise accelerates. Finally, if the curves touch, then the separatrix has \
a point of zero acceleration, but otherwise accelerates, and all other \
non-singular models have a decelerated expansion phase.

First run \[OpenCurlyQuote]Set-Up\[CloseCurlyQuote], then proceed to any \
other section.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.953806782945103*^9, 3.953806796746586*^9}, {
  3.960663064442158*^9, 3.9606632951904793`*^9}, {3.960663657855912*^9, 
  3.960663690448955*^9}, {3.960663809692812*^9, 3.960663822621565*^9}, {
  3.960663964996393*^9, 3.960664050605285*^9}, {3.960664084341357*^9, 
  3.960664100324112*^9}, {3.9609034758733215`*^9, 3.960903513767494*^9}, {
  3.960907101618332*^9, 3.960907227414299*^9}, {3.960907305693325*^9, 
  3.9609073756879463`*^9}, {3.9609079117289543`*^9, 
  3.9609079132099304`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e86f9561-6bac-ce4c-b2b6-\
6aae86f49a00"],

