Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Spiral Repellor 3D Phase Spaces",
 FontSize->24,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[0]]], "Chapter",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.8986756557416477`*^9, 3.898675658535152*^9}, {
   3.900736121951954*^9, 3.9007361666897087`*^9}, {3.900759488452829*^9, 
   3.900759491495037*^9}, {3.901008920130779*^9, 3.901008925531996*^9}, {
   3.901097307670695*^9, 3.9010973112706957`*^9}, 3.904980096358492*^9, 
   3.904980251240255*^9, {3.910440626117382*^9, 3.910440631445214*^9}, {
   3.917095447879121*^9, 3.917095449014579*^9}, {3.917095521432156*^9, 
   3.917095523076204*^9}, {3.9187240234834023`*^9, 3.9187240250606413`*^9}, {
   3.919151786886721*^9, 3.919151804744297*^9}, {3.9228718068670893`*^9, 
   3.922871807186956*^9}, 3.922872929964172*^9, {3.923561893839754*^9, 
   3.923561894568316*^9}, {3.923563347004373*^9, 3.923563351828269*^9}, 
   3.931343467909794*^9, {3.945246443900949*^9, 3.945246446603185*^9}, 
   3.9608993548932877`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"3aaa3ed0-ab58-4b8f-bc08-\
1588d6cec80b"],

Cell["\<\
Here we use the spiral repellor phase spaces as examples to show the full \
range of dynamics. The repellor and improper node cases have the same \
subcases, with the only difference being the stability of the high energy \
repellor fixed point.

The \[OpenCurlyQuote]curves\[CloseCurlyQuote] in the following headings refer \
to the separatrix and the zero acceleration (a\[CloseCurlyQuote]\
\[CloseCurlyQuote] = 0) curve, and correspond to the 2D phase spaces, where \
there are three subcases: the curves can cross each other, touch or do not \
cross.

In each case, we set X0 and Z0 to define a first integral surface in the 3D \
phase space. We also plot the projection of the X-Y-Z phase space on the X-Z \
plane, which shows the trajectory in the 2D phase space that corresponds to \
the first integral surface plotted in 3D. We find the location and stability \
of the Einstein fixed points along Y = 0, and plot closed trajectories in \
purple, and open trajectories in blue. The Flat Friedmann Separatrix (FFS) is \
plotted in green, which show the flat trajectories (k = 0), and the black \
curves show the Closed Friedmann Separatrix (CFS), which separates different \
types of closed models in the phase space. The orange fixed points in the 3D \
phase space are at the same X-Z location as the repellor in the 2D phase \
space, the dark blue fixed points are at the same X-Z location as the saddle, \
and the magenta fixed points are at the same X-Z location as the attractor. Y \
> 0 corresponds to expansion, and Y < 0 to contraction.

Trajectories which bounce once either contract from a low energy repelling \
fixed point (magenta), or a critical point representing a singularity \
(black), until they reach Y = 0 and bounce, then expand either to a low \
energy attractor (magenta) representing a cosmological constant, or to \
another critical point representing a singularity (black). Cyclic models have \
a bounce, and then expand until they reach a turnaround at Y = 0, where they \
re-collapse and bounce again at Y = 0. Expanding flat and open models emerge \
from a  non-singular fixed point (orange), and either expand towards a \
critical point representing a singularity (black), or to a low energy \
attractor (magenta).

Begin by running \[OpenCurlyQuote]Set-Up\[CloseCurlyQuote], followed by the \
corresponding \[OpenCurlyQuote]Solve the system with set parameter values\
\[CloseCurlyQuote] section, before running any other sections within each \
chapter.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.922872156918388*^9, 3.922872189827203*^9}, {
